Martin Read wrote:
> The problem is that Nethack is so complex that identifying all the
> places where things might need to be changed to adjust the game's
> difficulty is probably beyond reasonable feasibility.

I disagree with this statement.  To believe this is to believe that
Nethack can exist in no better form than in wihch it exists now.  It's
great, but it is not perfect.

While there are a very large number of things in Nethack, and it takes
a bit of effort to mold knowledge all its facts into a coherent mental
image of the game's design, it is by no means impossible.  Indeed, the
process of becoming a skilled player requires that one form just such
an image.

But I don't think ensuring players always know Identify (which could be
easier done by simply starting all players off with one identify
scroll) is a good idea.  The process of discovering that first scroll
is too important a part of the game.

